{"type":"text","text":"Done. Both routines are found, proven in the running emulator, recorded with `record_mapping` (source `trace and disassembly`), and promoted to the shared mapping.\n\n## 1. Lose a life on death — entry `$91CD`\n\n**Disassembly (`$91CD`–`$91E8`):**\n```\n$91CD: EE 74 07    inc $0774\n$91D0: A9 00       lda #$00\n$91D2: 8D 22 07    sta $0722\n$91D5: A9 80       lda #$80\n$91D7: 85 FC       sta $FC\n$91D9: CE 5A 07    dec $075A     <-- the instruction that takes the life\n$91DC: 10 0B       bpl $91E9     (still has lives -> next-life setup)\n$91DE: A9 00       lda #$00\n$91E0: 8D 72 07    sta $0772\n$91E3: A9 03       lda #$03\n$91E5: 8D 70 07    sta $0770     (game over flag)\n$91E8: 60          rts\n```\n\n**Evidence:** `trace_writes` on the lives counter `$075A` (while Mario ran into the first 1-1 Goomba) returned exactly one writer, **PC `$91DC`**, changing the value **2 → 1**. `$91DC` is the `BPL` immediately after the `DEC $075A` at `$91D9`. Walking back, the previous `RTS` is at `$91BC` and `$91BD`–`$91CC` is a data/jump-table region, so the routine's first instruction is `$91CD`. After the trace RAM `$075A` read 1 and the screenshot showed Mario respawned in 1-1. The only way to reach `$91D9` is straight-line fall-through from `$91CD` (no branch/jump target references it).\n\n**Caveat:** no direct `JSR`/`JMP` targets `$91CD` anywhere in PRG (searched `20 CD 91`, `4C CD 91`, and both byte orders), so it is reached via the game's indirect dispatch and I could not pin the caller. It is recorded as the entry per the \"instruction after the previous RTS\" rule.\n\n## 2. Count the level timer down — entry `$B74F` (caller `JSR $B74F` at `$AF35`)\n\n**Disassembly (`$B74F`–`$B7A3`, abridged):**\n```\n$B74F: AD 70 07    lda $0770\n$B752: F0 4F       beq $B7A3        (return unless timer active)\n$B754: A5 0E       lda $0E          (game-mode gate)\n...\n$B764: AD 87 07    lda $0787\n$B767: D0 3A       bne $B7A3        (still in the 24-frame divider)\n$B782: A9 40       lda #$40\n$B784: 85 FC       sta $FC          (hurry-up at 100)\n$B786: A9 18       lda #$18\n$B788: 8D 87 07    sta $0787        (divider = 24 frames)\n$B78B: A0 23       ldy #$23\n$B78D: A9 FF       lda #$FF         (signed -1)\n$B78F: 8D 39 01    sta $0139        <-- loads the subtract operand\n$B792: 20 5F 8F    jsr $8F5F        (shared BCD adder subtracts 1)\n$B795: A9 A4       lda #$A4\n$B797: 4C 06 8F    jmp $8F06        (tick sound)\n$B79A: 8D 56 07    sta $0756        (time-up path)\n$B79D: 20 31 D9    jsr $D931\n$B7A0: EE 59 07    inc $0759\n$B7A3: 60          rts\n```\n\n**Evidence:** `trace_writes` on the BCD operand byte `$0139` for 300 frames caught `$FF` writes from **PC `$B792`** exactly **12 times** — one tick every ~24 frames, matching the visible countdown. `$B792` is the `JSR` following `STA $0139` at `$B78F`. The routine entry is `$B74F` (previous `RTS` at `$B74A`, then data), and the caller is the `JSR $B74F` at **`$AF35`** (found by scanning PRG for `JSR $B7xx`).\n\n**Instruction that does the job:** `STA $0139 = $FF` at `$B78F` primes the shared helper `$8F5F` (already in the mapping, target `$07D7+$23` = `$07F8`–`$07FA`, the timer digits) with signed −1, so the helper performs the actual decrement each 24-frame tick.\n\nThe helper `$8F5F` (`AddBCD`) was already recorded; it is the shared adder for the coin, timer and score counters, so only its timer caller (`$B74F`) was added."}
